% Question 1
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\item On Homework 2, you developed an exact test for $E(X)=\mu_X=\lambda$
for data drawn from an exponential distribution with mean $\lambda$.

For context, we did this because the Central Limit Theorem,
\[T = \frac{\bar{X}-\lambda}{s/\sqrt{n}} \sim \mathcal{AT}(v=n-1),\]
can only be used when $n$ is large. That is, this approximation is 
``good" when $n$ is large. However, the \emph{exact} sampling distribution 
of $\bar{X}$,
\[\bar{X} \sim \textrm{Gamma}\left(\alpha=n, \beta=\frac{n}{\lambda}\right),\]
works regardless of $n$.

When asked to compute a $p$-value, many of you used the information you found
using a large language model. Namely, you used the suggested $p$-value 
calculation
\[p\textrm{-value} = 2 \times \min\left(F_{\bar{X}}(\bar{x}),1-F_{\bar{X}}(\bar{x})\right)\]
instead of simply directly computing
\begin{align*}
p\textrm{-value} &= P(\bar{X} \leq \lambda_0 - \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert)
+ P(\bar{X} \geq \lambda_0 + \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert)\\
&= P(\bar{X} \leq \lambda_0 - \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert)
+ (1 - P(\bar{X} \leq \lambda_0 + \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert))\\
&= F_{\bar{X}}(\lambda_0 - \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert)
+ (1-F_{\bar{X}}(\lambda_0 + \lvert \lambda_0 - \bar{x}\rvert)).
\end{align*}
My professor once told me ``you don't have to remember anything, you can just 
derive what you need." To be equally annoying, I will retool this to say ``you 
don't have to blindly trust anything, you can simulate it to check!"
\begin{enumerate}
\item Conduct a simulation to evaluate the probability of Type I error
using both approaches by completing the following in a loop. Report the 
resulting Type I error estimate.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Generate a random sample of $n=10$ observations from an exponential 
distribution with mean $\lambda=2$.
\item Compute $\Delta = \lvert \bar{x}-\lambda_0 \rvert$, where $\lambda_0=2$.
\item Compute the lower part of the $p$-value.
\[p_L = P(\bar{X} \leq \lambda_0 - \Delta)\]
\item Compute the upper part of the $p$-value.
\[p_U = P(\bar{X} \geq \lambda_0 + \Delta)\]
\item Compute the exact $p$-value.
\item Compute the $p$-value suggested by the large language model.
\end{enumerate}
<<message=F, warning=F, size ='scriptsize'>>=

set.seed(131313)

exact_rejection_rate <- rep(NA, 10000)
LLM_rejection_rate <- rep(NA, 10000)

n = 10
lambda = 2
lambda0 = 2

for (i in 1:10000){
  
  # part i
  rsamp <- rexp(n = n, rate = 1/lambda)
  
  # part ii
  delta = abs(mean(rsamp) - lambda0)
  
  #part iii
  pL = pgamma(q = lambda0-delta, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = T)
  
  # part iv
  pU = pgamma(q = lambda0+delta, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = F)
  
  #part v
  exact_p = pL + pU
  exact_rejection_rate[i] = ifelse(exact_p < 0.05, 1, 0)
  
  #part vi
  LLM_lower = pgamma(mean(rsamp), shape = n, rate = n/lambda0)
  LLM_upper = 1 - pgamma(mean(rsamp), shape = n, rate = n/lambda0)
  LLM_p = 2*min(LLM_lower, LLM_upper)
  LLM_rejection_rate[i] = ifelse(LLM_p < 0.05, 1, 0)
  
}

(mean(exact_rejection_rate))

(mean(LLM_rejection_rate))

@

\textcolor{red}{The $\alpha$ of the exact method is 0.0498. The $\alpha$ of the LLM approach is 0.0479}

\item Conduct a simulated power analysis to evaluate the power and 
probability of Type II error using both approaches by completing the 
following in a loop. Report the resulting Power estimate.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Generate a random sample of $n=10$ observations from an exponential 
distribution with mean $\lambda=2.3$.
\item Compute $\Delta = \lvert \bar{x}-\lambda_0 \rvert$, where $\lambda_0=2$.
\item Compute the lower part of the $p$-value.
\[p_L = P(\bar{X} \leq \lambda_0 - \Delta)\]
\item Compute the upper part of the $p$-value.
\[p_U = P(\bar{X} \geq \lambda_0 + \Delta)\]
\item Compute the exact $p$-value.
\item Compute the $p$-value suggested by the large language model.

<<warning=F, message=F, size ='scriptsize'>>=

exact_power <- rep(NA, 10000)
LLM_power <- rep(NA, 10000)

n = 10
lambda = 2.3
lambda0 = 2

for (i in 1:10000){
  
  # part i
  rsamp <- rexp(n = n, rate = 1/lambda)
  
  # part ii
  delta = abs(mean(rsamp) - lambda0)
  
  #part iii
  pL = pgamma(q = lambda0-delta, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = T)
  
  # part iv
  pU = pgamma(q = lambda0+delta, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = F)
  
  #part v
  exact_p = pL + pU
  exact_power[i] = ifelse(exact_p < 0.05, 1, 0)
  
  #part vi
  LLM_lower = pgamma(mean(rsamp), shape = n, rate = n/lambda0)
  LLM_upper = 1 - pgamma(mean(rsamp), shape = n, rate = n/lambda0)
  LLM_p = 2*min(LLM_lower, LLM_upper)
  LLM_power[i] = ifelse(LLM_p < 0.05, 1, 0)
  
}

(mean(exact_power))

(mean(LLM_power))

@

\textcolor{red}{The power of our exact method is 0.1125. The power of the LLM approximation is 0.0853.}

\end{enumerate}
\item To gain insight into your results, compute the exact $p$-value and
the $p$-value suggested by the large language model for a fine grid of
values of $\Delta$ from 0 to 4 for $\lambda_0=2$. Plot the resulting $p$-values against
a $y=x$ line. Explain how the visual maps onto the results of the simulations.

<<p_value_plot, message=F, warning=F, eval=F, size ='scriptsize'>>=

lambda0 = 2
n = 10

delta <- seq(0,4,length.out = 1000)

exact_p <- rep(NA, 1000)

LLM_p_high <- rep(NA, 1000)
LLM_p_low <- rep(NA, 1000)

for(i in 1:1000){
  
  #exact p-value calculation
  exact_lower <- pgamma(lambda0-delta[i], shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = T)
  exact_upper <- pgamma(lambda0+delta[i], shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = F)
  exact_p[i] = exact_lower + exact_upper
  
  #LLM p-value calculation
  xbar_approx_low = lambda0-delta[i]
  xbar_approx_high = lambda0 + delta[i]
  LLM_p_low[i] = 2 * pgamma(xbar_approx_low, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = T)
  LLM_p_high[i] = 2 * pgamma(xbar_approx_high, shape = n, rate = n/lambda0, lower.tail = F)
  
}


library(tidyverse)
library(ggplot2) 

p_values <- tibble(exact = exact_p, 
                   LLM_low = LLM_p_low, 
                   LLM_high = LLM_p_high)


ggplot(data = p_values) + 
  geom_point(aes(x = exact, y = LLM_low, color = "Low Estimate")) + 
  geom_point(aes(x = exact, y = LLM_high, color = "High Estimate")) +
  geom_abline(intercept = 0, slope = 1, color = "black", linetype = "dashed") + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0) +
  theme_bw() +
  xlab('Exact p-value') +
  ylab('LLM p-value') +
  ggtitle('Exact vs. LLM Approach') +
  theme(legend.title=element_blank()) + 
  xlim(0,1) + 
  ylim(0,1)


@

\begin{figure}[H]

<<fig.dim = c(7,4), echo=F, warning=F, message=F, size ='scriptsize'>>= 

<<p_value_plot>>

@


\label{p-value_plot}
\caption{p-value Comparison}

\end{figure}

\item Rerun the simulations when $n=1000$. Just rerun your code above with
the sample size changed and report the results here -- no need to copy and
paste the code again here. Provide the results and comment on how they have
changed from $n=10$. What did students who used the large language model 
unwittingly assume, and is it problematic?
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{red}{As the sample size increases to $n = 1000$, the two methods are virtually identical in terms of $\alpha$ and Power. Students who used the LLM version of the calculation assumed that the LLM method would be approximately robust even with a low sample size, as the exact version is. This can be problematic as most datasets used in statistics are not as large as $n = 1000$, so the difference in $\alpha$ and power could make a difference in the hypothesis test.}
