\item Under the alternative hypothesis, the distribution of the ANOVA $F$ 
statistic follows a non-central $F$ distribution. An added non-centrality 
parameter ($\lambda$) shifts the mean of the usual $F$ distribution away 
from 1 (what we expect under $H_0$). Use this fact to conduct a power analysis 
for the ANOVA test.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $N=542$ and $k=5$ based on the MFAP4 example from class. Find the 
$F$ statistic that starts the rejection region for the usual ANOVA test.\\
\textbf{Hint:} The degrees of freedom are $df_1=k-1$, $df_2=N-k=k(n-1)$when the sample sizes are the same.

<<warning=F, message=F, size ='scriptsize'>>=

N = 542
k = 5

(rejection_statistic <- qf(0.95, df1 = k-1, df2 = N-k, lower.tail = T))

@
\item Convert the effect size of the ANOVA test -- $\omega^2 = 0.23$ for the 
MFAP4 data -- to Cohen's $f$ using the following formula.
\[\text{Cohen's}~f = \sqrt{\frac{\omega^2}{1-\omega^2}}\]

<<size ='scriptsize'>>=

omega2 <- 0.23

(cohensf <- sqrt(omega2/(1-omega2)))

@
\item Let $N=542$ and $k=5$ based on the MFAP4 example from class. Use 
Cohen's $f$ from the previous part to compute the non-centrality parameter.
\begin{align*}
\lambda &=N \times (\textrm{Cohen's}~f)^2 \\
&=k \times n \times (\textrm{Cohen's}~f)^2 \tag{Equal sample size $n$}
\end{align*}

<<size ='scriptsize'>>=

(lambda = N * cohensf^2)

@
\item Let $n=542$ and $k=5$ based on the MFAP4 example from class. What is 
the probability that we observe the $F$ statistic in (a) under the $F$ 
distribution with $df_1=k-1$, $df_2=N-k=k(n-1)$ or one that provides more
support for the alternative when the sample sizes are the same, and 
non-centrality parameter $\lambda$ from the previous part?

<<size ='scriptsize'>>=

n = 542
k = 5

pf(q=rejection_statistic, df1 = k-1, df2 = n-k, ncp = lambda, lower.tail = F)

@

\item Write a function that takes $k$, and $\omega^2$ and computes the power:
\[P\left(F \geq F_{k-1, k(n-1), \lambda=0}|k-1, k(n-1), \lambda=\lambda\right).\]
\textbf{Hint:} A loop should be helpful here.

<<size ='scriptsize'>>=

power <- function(k, omega2, desired_power){
  
  cohensf <- sqrt(omega2/(1-omega2))
  
  n = 3
  
  repeat{
    
    rejection <- rep(NA, 1000)
    
    lambda = n * k * cohensf^2
    
    rejection_stat = qf(0.95, df1 = k-1, df2 = k*(n-1))
    
    p = pf(q = rejection_stat, df1 = k-1, df2 = k*(n-1), ncp = lambda, lower.tail = F)
    
    if(p >= desired_power){
      return(n)
      break
    }else{
      n = n + 1
    }
    
  }
}

@

\item Use the function in the previous part to find what $n$ has to be to 
ensure this probability is 80\% when $k=5$ and $\omega^2=0.14$ (a moderate 
effect)?\\
\textbf{Hint:} You can check your answer using \texttt{pwr::pwr.anova.test()}.

<<warning=F, message=F, size ='scriptsize'>>=

power(k = 5, omega2 = 0.14, desired_power = 0.8)

pwr::pwr.anova.test(k = 5, power = 0.8, f = sqrt(0.14/(1-0.14)))

@

\textcolor{red}{To ensure 80\% power, you'd need a group size n of at least 16.}

\item For the sample size determined in the previous part, plot the 
distribution of $F$ under the null and under the alternative specified 
by $\omega^2=0.14$.

\begin{figure}[H]

<<warning=F, message=F, fig.dim=c(6,3), size ='scriptsize'>>=

library(tidyverse)

n = 16

omega2 = 0.14

cohensf <- sqrt(omega2/(1-omega2))

lambda = n * k * cohensf^2

ggdat <- tibble(x = seq(0, 12, length.out = 10000), 
                h0 = df(x = x, df1 = k-1, df2 = k*(n-1)), 
                ha = df(x = x, df1 = k-1, df2 = k*(n-1), ncp = lambda))

ggplot(data = ggdat) + 
  geom_point(aes(x = x, y = h0, color = "Null")) + 
  geom_point(aes(x = x, y = ha, color = "Alternative")) + 
  theme_bw() + 
  xlab('F') + 
  ylab('Density') +
  ggtitle('Null vs. Alternative F Distribution') + 
  theme(legend.title=element_blank()) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0)

@

\label{F_distribution_plots}
\caption{Comparison of F Distributions}

\end{figure}

\end{enumerate}
