% Question 3
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\item \cite{Garofalo23} investigated how the immune system communicates with 
the brain in mice. Specifically, they genetically modified or treated mice to 
block certain immune cells or signaling pathways.
\begin{itemize}
\item The aNK1.1 mice have depleted natural killer (NK) cells, which form an 
important part of the immune system.
\item The aVLA4 mice have the function of a protein called VLA-4 blocked, 
which inhibits the effect of NK cells on the brain.
\item Rag2$-/-$ mice are genetically modified to lack mature T and B cells, 
and have NK cells.
\item Rag2$-/-\gamma$c$-/-$ mice are genetically modified to lack T, B, and 
NK cells.
\item IgG2a and IgG2b are control groups.
\end{itemize}
In a open-field test, each mouse was placed in an enclosed platform (40 cm 
$\times$ 40 cm $\times$ 30cm) for 10 minutes. The researchers recorded the 
distance traveled, the movement duration, and time spent in the center area 
(20 cm $\times$ 20 cm).

The researchers hypothesized that the mice with inhibited immune system 
functionality would exhibit increased explorative behavior. This would suggest 
reduced anxiety-like responses to open spaces. I have provided data about 
distance traveled in meters, do these data support their hypothesis?\\
\textbf{Hint 1:} You will have to \texttt{pivot\_longer()} the data I copied 
from their posted source data.\\
\textbf{Hint 2:} Their data do not appear to match the data plotted in their
figure for at least the Rag2$-/-\gamma$c$-/-$ group. I've emailed the author 
for updated data (holds breath). Continue as if the data are correct.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[H]

<<warning=F, message=F, fig.dim=c(10,4), size ='scriptsize'>>=

dat <- read_csv('Garofalo.csv')

dat <- dat |>
  rename(control_1 = IgG2a, 
         depleted_nk = aNK1.1, 
         control_2 = IgG2b, 
         blocked_vla = `aVLA-4`, 
         lack_T_B = `Rag2-/-`, 
         lack_T_B_nk = `Rag2-/-gc-/-`) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = everything(), values_to = "distance", names_to = "treatment")

norm <- ggplot(data = dat, aes(x = treatment, y = distance)) + 
  geom_violin(width = 1) + 
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.25) + 
  theme_bw() + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0) + 
  xlab('Treatment Group') + 
  ylab('Distance Travelled') + 
  ggtitle('Distance Travelled by Treatment Group')

logged <- ggplot(data = dat, aes(x = treatment, y = log2(distance))) + 
  geom_violin(width = 1) + 
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.25) + 
  theme_bw() + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0) + 
  xlab('Treatment Group') + 
  ylab('Distance Travelled (log2)') + 
  ggtitle('Log2 Transformed')

library(patchwork)

norm + logged

@


\label{mouse_summary}
\caption{Distance Travelled by Mice Across Treatments}

\end{figure}

The data appears to be more normally distributed under a $log_{2}$ transformation, and it helps with outliers in several of the groups. Also, the variances are clearly not similar across groups so we'll be using a Welch's ANOVA, which does not assume equal variances across groups. 

<<message=F, warning=F, size='scriptsize'>>=

library(rstatix)
(aov.test <- welch_anova_test(data = dat, log2(distance) ~ treatment))


@

After performing a Welch's ANOVA on our $log_{2}$ transformed data, we obtain a test statistic $F = 12.7$, $p \textless 0.0001$. Because $p \textless 0.05 = \alpha$, we have indication that the true population means of distance traveled among the treatment groups are not all equal. 


<<message=F, warning=F, size='scriptsize'>>= 

dat$log2 <- log2(dat$distance)

(games_howell_test(data = dat, log2 ~ treatment))

@

After performing a Games-Howell pairwise follow-up to Welch's ANOVA, we can see that there are statistically discernible differences of $log_{2}$ transformed means between 5 of the pairwise comparisons:

\begin{enumerate}

\item{Mice with the blocked VLA-4 protein travel greater $log_{2}$ transformed distance than mice that lack T, B, and natural killer cells: 95 $\%$ CI [0.33, 1.37] ($p = 0.001$).}

\item{Mice that lack T, B, and natural killer cells travel less $log_{2}$ transformed distance than control group 1 mice: 95$\%$ CI [-2.74, -0.68] ($p = 0.001$).}

\item{Mice that lack T, B, and natural killer cells travel less $log_{2}$ transformed distance than control group 2 mice: 95$\%$ CI [-2.39, -0.61] ($p = 0.002$).}

\item{Mice that lack T, B, and natural killer cells travel less $log_{2}$ transformed distance than mice that have depleted natural killer cells: 95$\%$ CI [-1.46, -0.43] ($p \textless 0.001$).}

\item{Finally, mice that lack T, B, and natural killer cells travel less $log_{2}$ transformed distance than mice that lack T and B cells: 95$\%$ CI [-1.39, -0.42] ($p \textless 0.001$).}

\end{enumerate}


